well all this week we're looking at  healthy habits for the new year you know  you always hear me encourage you to  drink more water in our dry climate but  it's not just a matter of quenching your  thirst no water is vital for our overall  well-being in so many ways and knowing  the power that it holds for a healthy  body and mind will likely change your  drinking habits starting maybe today  do you think about drinking water only  when you're thirsty it may surprise you  to know that many parts of our body that  depend on h2o to function may already be  on the way to dehydration by then up to  60 percent of our body weight is water  all of our vital organs especially our  brains hold even more and babies believe  it or not are up to eighty percent water  water feeds our body's cells to rebuild  and regulates our internal temperature  through sweat and breathing it  lubricates our joints and acts as a  shock absorber for our brain and spinal  cord we can't produce saliva without  water it metabolizes and carries the  food we eat through our bloodstream and  then works to flush waste and toxins out  even mild dehydration can impair your  mood your memory and your brain  performance headaches confusion muscle  cramps and constipation can all be  thanks to too little h2o  so how much water do we need to perform  at our peak every day an average adult  should drink between two and three  quarts of water that's 64 to 96 ounces  more if you're an aggressive exerciser  it's not unusual to lose one to three  percent of body weight through normal  daily activities exercise or high heat  which we certainly get a lot of here in  the desert
 

